--- !u!114 &714271383
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 714271381}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 7fd8192303e754ff28370bd634bbb690, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  allLevels:
  - question: Which of the following is NOT a primary purpose of endotracheal tube
      (ETT) placement?
    option1: a) Keeping the airway open
    option2: b) Mechanical ventilation
    option3: c) Administration of anesthesia
    option4: d) Remove airway blockages
    correctAnswer: c) Administration of anesthesia
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: With reference to the carina, where should the tip of the ETT be positioned
      on a chest radiograph in an adult, given that the neck is in a neutral position?
    option1: a) 2 cm above the carina
    option2: b) 5 cm above the carina
    option3: c) 9 cm above the carina
    option4: d) 1.5 cm above the carina
    correctAnswer: b) 5 cm above the carina
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Which of the following is a complication of ETT placement?
    option1: a) Pneumothorax
    option2: b) Laryngeal injury
    option3: c) Tracheal injury
    option4: d) All of the above
    correctAnswer: d) All of the above
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: When using the thoracic vertebrae as reference, the tip of the ETT
      should lie over T2 to __ ?
    option1: a) T3
    option2: b) T4
    option3: d) T5
    option4: d) T6
    correctAnswer: b) T4
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Which of the following is a contraindication for ETT placement?
    option1: a) Severe cervical spine injury
    option2: b) Difficult airway anatomy
    option3: c) Increased intracranial pressure
    option4: d) All of the above
    correctAnswer: d) All of the above
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Which of the following is NOT an advantage of a cuffed ETT over an
      uncuffed ETT?
    option1: a) Improved ventilation
    option2: b) Reduced risk of aspiration
    option3: c) Better seal of the airway
    option4: d) Reduced resistance to airflow
    correctAnswer: d) Reduced resistance to airflow
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: To diagnose inadvertent esophageal intubation, what type of chest radiograph
      can be taken to confirm the location of the ETT outside the trachea and within
      the esophagus?
    option1: a) Anteroposterior (AP)
    option2: b) Posteroanterior (PA)
    option3: c) Right posterior oblique
    option4: d) Lateral
    correctAnswer: c) Right posterior oblique
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Which of the following is a radiographic sign of a properly placed
      ETT?
    option1: a) Overdistended cuffs
    option2: b) Tube tip in the left/right main bronchus
    option3: c) Tube tip in the middle third of the trachea
    option4: d) Tube tip in the upper third of the trachea
    correctAnswer: c) Tube tip in the middle third of the trachea
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Which of the following is/are indications for a CVC placement?
    option1: a) Venous access for fluid administration
    option2: b) Monitoring of central venous pressure
    option3: c) Delivering parenteral nutrition
    option4: d) All of the above
    correctAnswer: d) All of the above
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Where is/are the general ideal tip position(s) for a CVC?
    option1: a) Superior vena cava
    option2: b) Inferior vena cava
    option3: c) Cavoatrial junction
    option4: d) Both a and c
    correctAnswer: d) Both a and c
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Which of the following is NOT a common complication of CVC placement?
    option1: a) Pneumothorax
    option2: b) Pulmonary edema
    option3: c) Catheter embolism
    option4: d) Arterial puncture
    correctAnswer: b) Pulmonary edema
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: What is the most appropriate radiographic view to confirm the position
      of a CVC?
    option1: a) Anteroposterior (AP) chest X-ray
    option2: b) Lateral chest X-ray
    option3: c) Sternum oblique chest X-ray
    option4: d) Posteroanterior (PA) chest X-ray
    correctAnswer: a) Anteroposterior (AP) chest X-ray
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Which of the following is an absolute contraindication for CVC placement?
    option1: a) Coagulopathy
    option2: b) Active skin/ soft tissue infection at the potential placement site
    option3: c) Severe bleeding disorders
    option4: d) Uncooperative patient
    correctAnswer: b) Active skin/ soft tissue infection at the potential placement
      site
    isAnswered: 0
  - question: Using the clavicle as a landmark, where should the needle be inserted
      when administering an internal jugular vein CVC?
    option1: a) 5 cm superior to the clavicle
    option2: b) 5 cm inferior to the clavicle
    option3: c) 2.5 cm inferior to the clavicle
    option4: d) 2.5 cm superior to the clavicle
